# yaml-language-server: $schema=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-avc/openavc-drivers/main/avcdriver.schema.json
#
# SMART Board T Pro interactive displays (network control protocol)
# Reference: SMART Board T Pro series "Appendix D: Network control protocol
#            codes" (docs.smarttech.com/kb/172095, 15 December 2025), the
#            support page "Managing the display using network control
#            protocol", and the T Pro installation and maintenance guide
#            (docs.smarttech.com/kb/172043, 16 December 2025), whose Appendix
#            A lists the Settings app and Appendix C the network setup.
# Protocol: fixed ASCII frames over TCP 4664, no login, each ended with CR.
#             Set  ":01S" <code> <3-character value>  answered "401+" (accepted)
#                                                    or "401-" (refused)
#             Get  ":01G" <code> "000"                answered ":01r" <code> <value>
#           The code is one character (":" is Video Source, so a source set
#           reads ":01S:001"). Signed values (treble, bass, balance) are a
#           sign and two digits, "+06" / "-06". SMART asks for each reply
#           before the next frame is sent and says the display may accept a
#           value it does not support and change nothing. The frame layout
#           and the "401+" acknowledgement are the ViewSonic LFD protocol's
#           (viewsonic_cde), with ":" in place of the length byte and capital
#           S / G.
# Push: none. "The display will not send packets autonomously." Everything is
#       polled (10 s); a set's answer carries no value, and SMART asks for
#       one frame at a time, so a command does not send a read-back get with
#       it and its new value appears at the next poll.
# Power: READY (screen off, network control works, Power On turns it on;
#        "Transition to ON only works while in READY state"), ON, and
#        POWERSAVE ("While in POWERSAVE, network control does not function";
#        the Power button, the remote or a video signal wakes it). A get does
#        not wake the display, so the poll runs in every power state, and in
#        POWERSAVE the platform's silence check reports the display offline
#        (no_response) until it answers again.
# Eco mode: per the code list's footnote, turning Eco mode on or off turns
#           the display's Ethernet off, so network control ends until someone
#           enables Ethernet at the display. The driver reads it and never
#           writes it.
# Not covered: the display's RS-232 port speaks the text "get / set" grammar
#              of the other SMART Boards (with push and identity reads), not
#              these frames, so a serial T Pro needs its own driver.
#
# Why YAML: fixed text frames ended with CR, one reply per frame, each reply
# naming the code it answers, which ConfigurableDriver reads with one rule per
# code.
#
# Not verified on hardware (no T Pro on the bench). Unverified from the
# documents alone: how the display writes a signed zero (the driver sends
# "+00"), which of the two microphone-array codes (47 and 51, described
# identically) controls what, the Balance code (the code column and the get
# say 0x33, both set examples print 0x35; the driver uses 0x33), whether the
# display keeps one TCP connection open between frames, and what it answers
# to a get while it is changing power state.

id: smart_board_t_pro
name: SMART Board T Pro (Network)
manufacturer: SMART Technologies
category: display
version: 1.0.0
author: OpenAVC
description: >-
  SMART Board T Pro interactive displays over the network (TCP port 4664):
  power, input, volume, mute, the microphone array, the display's remote
  keys, and its picture, sound, touch and timer settings.
source_url: https://docs.smarttech.com/kb/172095
tags: [display, smart, smart-board, interactive-display, interactive-flat-panel, education, lan]
verified: false
simulated: true
ports: [4664]
min_platform_version: "0.37.0"
compatible_models:
  - manufacturer: SMART Technologies
    models: [SBID-T065-P, SBID-T075-P, SBID-T086-P]
    confidence: untested
    notes: >-
      SMART Board T Pro series (IDT65-1 / IDT75-1 / IDT86-1). Network control on TCP 4664. The
      RS-232 port speaks a different protocol, which this driver does not use.
protocols: [smart_board_network]
transport: tcp
delimiter: "\r"

help:
  overview: >-
    SMART Board T Pro interactive displays over the network: power, input,
    volume, mute, the microphone array and the display's remote keys, plus
    treble, bass, balance, contrast, backlight, color temperature, language,
    speakers, touch, the screen timer, automatic brightness and the IR remote
    sensor as device settings. The display does not report changes by itself,
    so everything is read every 10 seconds.
  setup: >-
    1. Connect either of the display's RJ45 jacks to the network. On the
       display, press Input, tap Network, and note the IP address (reserve
       it in DHCP or set a static address). Ethernet must be Enabled in the
       display's Network settings.

    2. Add the display here with that address. The port is 4664 and there
       is no login.

    3. Keep the display out of Power Save. In Power Save the display does
       not answer on the network, and only its Power button, the remote or a
       video signal wakes it. Power Off (Ready) turns the screen off and keeps
       network control working.

    4. Changing Eco mode turns the display's Ethernet off. After changing it
       at the display, turn Ethernet back on in the display's Network
       settings.

    5. This driver is for network control. The display's RS-232 port uses a
       different set of commands.
  connection: >-
    The display answers on the network while it is on or in Ready (screen
    off). In Power Save it answers nothing, so OpenAVC shows it offline until
    someone presses its Power button, uses the remote, or connects a video
    source.

default_config:
  host: ""
  port: 4664
  poll_interval: 10
  inter_command_delay: 0.2

config_schema:
  host:
    type: string
    required: true
    label: IP Address
  port:
    type: integer
    default: 4664
    label: Port
    description: 4664 on the T Pro.
  poll_interval:
    type: integer
    default: 10
    min: 0
    label: Poll Interval (sec)
    description: How often to read the display's settings. The display does not report changes by itself, so a change made at the display appears at the next read. 0 turns polling off.
  inter_command_delay:
    type: number
    default: 0.2
    min: 0
    label: Inter-Command Delay (sec)
    description: Gap between commands. SMART asks for each reply before the next command is sent; raise this if the display misses commands.

state_variables:
  power:
    type: enum
    values: ["on", ready, powersave]
    label: Power
    control: true
    help: "on = picture up. ready = screen off, and Power On turns it on. powersave = lowest power: the display does not answer on the network until its Power button, the remote or a video signal wakes it."
  input:
    type: enum
    values: [hdmi1, hdmi2, hdmi3, ops1, usbc1, usbc2]
    label: Input
    control: true
    help: "The active input: hdmi1 to hdmi3, ops1 (the slot-in PC), usbc1 or usbc2."
  volume:
    type: integer
    label: Volume
    min: 0
    max: 30
    step: 1
    control: true
  mute:
    type: boolean
    label: Mute
    control: true
    help: The speakers are muted.
  mic_array:
    type: boolean
    label: Microphone Array
    control: true
    help: The display's microphone array is on (code 47 in SMART's list).
  mic_array_alt:
    type: boolean
    label: Microphone Array (code 51)
    help: SMART's code list has a second microphone-array code, 51, with the same description as 47.
  treble:
    type: integer
    label: Treble
    min: -6
    max: 6
    step: 1
  bass:
    type: integer
    label: Bass
    min: -6
    max: 6
    step: 1
  balance:
    type: integer
    label: Balance
    min: -50
    max: 50
    step: 1
    help: Left / right balance of the built-in speakers. Negative is left.
  contrast:
    type: integer
    label: Contrast
    min: 0
    max: 100
    step: 1
  backlight:
    type: integer
    label: Backlight
    min: 0
    max: 100
    step: 1
  color_temperature:
    type: enum
    values: [very_cool, cool, neutral, warm, very_warm]
    label: Color Temperature
  language:
    type: enum
    values: [english, french, spanish, chinese_simplified, german, dutch, danish, italian, norwegian, finnish]
    label: Language
  speaker_output:
    type: enum
    values: ["off", "on", auto, subwoofer_off, subwoofer_on]
    label: Speaker Output
    help: "The built-in speakers: off, on, or auto (off while an external audio device is connected). SMART's code list puts the subwoofer switch on the same code."
  touch:
    type: boolean
    label: Touch
    help: Touch input is on.
  screen_timeout:
    type: enum
    values: [never, 1min, 5min, 10min, 30min, 60min, 90min, 5h, 10h]
    label: Screen Timeout
    help: How long the display waits before turning the screen off.
  auto_brightness:
    type: boolean
    label: Automatic Brightness
    help: The display adjusts its brightness to the light in the space.
  ir_remote:
    type: boolean
    label: IR Remote
    help: The display takes commands from its infrared remote.
  eco_mode:
    type: boolean
    label: Eco Mode
    help: Eco mode is on (lower screen brightness). Read only. Changing it turns the display's Ethernet off, so change it at the display and turn Ethernet back on afterwards.
  last_error:
    type: string
    label: Last Error
    help: Set when the display refuses a command or a read (a value it does not take, or a setting this model does not have). Cleared by the next poll that the display answers in full.

commands:
  # A set's answer is "401+" with no value, so no command reads its value
  # back; sets: is what the simulator and the device audit check, and the
  # poll reads the display's own value.
  power_on:
    label: Power On
    help: Turns the display on from Ready. In Power Save the display does not answer on the network.
    send: ":01S0001\r"
    sets: { power: "on" }
  power_off:
    label: Power Off (Ready)
    help: Turns the screen off and leaves the display in Ready, where it still answers on the network and Power On turns it back on.
    send: ":01S0000\r"
    sets: { power: ready }
  power_save:
    label: Power Save
    help: Puts the display in its lowest-power state. It then stops answering on the network and shows offline until its Power button, the remote or a video signal wakes it.
    send: ":01S0002\r"
    sets: { power: powersave }
    confirm: The display stops answering on the network. OpenAVC cannot turn it back on; only its Power button, the remote or a video signal wakes it.

  set_input:
    label: Select Input
    help: Selects an input.
    send: ":01S:{input}\r"
    sets: { input: "{input}" }
    params:
      input:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Input
        values:
          - { value: hdmi1, label: "HDMI 1" }
          - { value: hdmi2, label: "HDMI 2" }
          - { value: hdmi3, label: "HDMI 3" }
          - { value: ops1, label: "Slot-in PC (OPS)" }
          - { value: usbc1, label: "USB-C 1" }
          - { value: usbc2, label: "USB-C 2" }
        map: { hdmi1: "001", hdmi2: "002", hdmi3: "021", ops1: "103", usbc1: "104", usbc2: "105" }

  set_volume:
    label: Set Volume
    help: Sets the speaker volume, 0 to 30.
    send: ":01S8{level:03d}\r"
    sets: { volume: "{level}" }
    params:
      level:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Volume
        min: 0
        max: 30
  volume_up:
    label: Volume Up
    help: Raises the volume one step, like the remote's Volume + key.
    send: ":01SA000\r"
  volume_down:
    label: Volume Down
    help: Lowers the volume one step, like the remote's Volume - key.
    send: ":01SA001\r"
  mute_on:
    label: Mute On
    help: Mutes the speakers.
    send: ":01S9001\r"
    sets: { mute: true }
  mute_off:
    label: Mute Off
    help: Unmutes the speakers.
    send: ":01S9000\r"
    sets: { mute: false }

  mic_array_on:
    label: Microphone Array On
    help: Turns the display's microphone array on (code 47).
    send: ":01SG001\r"
    sets: { mic_array: true }
  mic_array_off:
    label: Microphone Array Off
    help: Turns the display's microphone array off (code 47). If the microphones stay on, try the Microphone Array (code 51) setting on the device page.
    send: ":01SG000\r"
    sets: { mic_array: false }

  remote_key:
    label: Remote Key
    help: Presses a key on the display's remote. Freeze, Mute and Screen Shade switch between on and off with each press; the display does not report freeze or screen shade over the network.
    send: ":01SA{key}\r"
    params:
      key:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Key
        values:
          - { value: volume_up, label: "Volume +" }
          - { value: volume_down, label: "Volume -" }
          - { value: up, label: Up }
          - { value: down, label: Down }
          - { value: left, label: Left }
          - { value: right, label: Right }
          - { value: enter, label: Enter }
          - { value: menu, label: "Menu / Settings" }
          - { value: input, label: Input }
          - { value: back, label: "Back / Exit" }
          - { value: freeze, label: Freeze }
          - { value: mute, label: Mute }
          - { value: home, label: Home }
          - { value: screen_shade, label: Screen Shade }
        map:
          volume_up: "000"
          volume_down: "001"
          up: "010"
          down: "011"
          left: "012"
          right: "013"
          enter: "014"
          menu: "020"
          input: "021"
          back: "022"
          freeze: "032"
          mute: "033"
          home: "034"
          screen_shade: "100"

  factory_reset:
    label: Factory Reset
    help: Resets the display to its factory settings.
    send: ":01ST001\r"
    confirm: Erases every setting on the display, its network settings included, and returns it to its factory state. OpenAVC may lose its connection to it.

# Eco mode and Power Save are commands or reads, never settings: a setting
# queued while the display is offline is sent again on reconnect, and both
# end network control.
device_settings:
  treble:
    type: integer
    label: Treble
    help: Treble, -6 to 6.
    state_key: treble
    default: 0
    setup: false
    min: -6
    max: 6
    write:
      send: ":01S1{value:+03d}\r"
  bass:
    type: integer
    label: Bass
    help: Bass, -6 to 6.
    state_key: bass
    default: 0
    setup: false
    min: -6
    max: 6
    write:
      send: ":01S2{value:+03d}\r"
  balance:
    type: integer
    label: Balance
    help: Left / right balance of the built-in speakers, -50 (left) to 50 (right).
    state_key: balance
    default: 0
    setup: false
    min: -50
    max: 50
    write:
      send: ":01S3{value:+03d}\r"
  contrast:
    type: integer
    label: Contrast
    help: Contrast, 0 to 100.
    state_key: contrast
    default: 50
    setup: false
    min: 0
    max: 100
    write:
      send: ":01S4{value:03d}\r"
  backlight:
    type: integer
    label: Backlight
    help: Backlight level, 0 to 100.
    state_key: backlight
    default: 100
    setup: false
    min: 0
    max: 100
    write:
      send: ":01S?{value:03d}\r"
  color_temperature:
    type: enum
    label: Color Temperature
    help: Color temperature of the picture.
    state_key: color_temperature
    default: neutral
    setup: false
    values:
      - { value: very_cool, label: Very Cool }
      - { value: cool, label: Cool }
      - { value: neutral, label: Neutral }
      - { value: warm, label: Warm }
      - { value: very_warm, label: Very Warm }
    map: { cool: "000", neutral: "001", warm: "002", very_cool: "003", very_warm: "004" }
    write:
      send: ":01S@{value}\r"
  language:
    type: enum
    label: Language
    help: The language of the display's menus.
    state_key: language
    default: english
    setup: false
    values:
      - { value: english, label: English }
      - { value: french, label: French }
      - { value: spanish, label: Spanish }
      - { value: chinese_simplified, label: Simplified Chinese }
      - { value: german, label: German }
      - { value: dutch, label: Dutch }
      - { value: danish, label: Danish }
      - { value: italian, label: Italian }
      - { value: norwegian, label: Norwegian }
      - { value: finnish, label: Finnish }
    map:
      english: "000"
      french: "001"
      spanish: "002"
      chinese_simplified: "004"
      german: "006"
      dutch: "007"
      danish: "011"
      italian: "013"
      norwegian: "015"
      finnish: "016"
    write:
      send: ":01S<{value}\r"
  speaker_output:
    type: enum
    label: Speaker Output
    help: "The built-in speakers: Off, On, or Auto (off while an external audio device is connected), or the subwoofer off or on."
    state_key: speaker_output
    default: auto
    setup: false
    values:
      - { value: "off", label: "Off" }
      - { value: "on", label: "On" }
      - { value: auto, label: Auto }
      - { value: subwoofer_off, label: Subwoofer Off }
      - { value: subwoofer_on, label: Subwoofer On }
    map: { "off": "000", "on": "001", "auto": "002", "subwoofer_off": "003", "subwoofer_on": "004" }
    write:
      send: ":01SC{value}\r"
  touch:
    type: boolean
    label: Touch
    help: Turns touch input on or off.
    state_key: touch
    default: true
    setup: false
    map: { "true": "001", "false": "000" }
    write:
      send: ":01SD{value}\r"
  screen_timeout:
    type: enum
    label: Screen Timeout
    help: How long the display waits before turning the screen off.
    state_key: screen_timeout
    default: never
    setup: false
    values:
      - { value: never, label: Never }
      - { value: 1min, label: 1 minute }
      - { value: 5min, label: 5 minutes }
      - { value: 10min, label: 10 minutes }
      - { value: 30min, label: 30 minutes }
      - { value: 60min, label: 60 minutes }
      - { value: 90min, label: 90 minutes }
      - { value: 5h, label: 5 hours }
      - { value: 10h, label: 10 hours }
    map: { never: "000", 1min: "001", 5min: "005", 10min: "010", 30min: "030", 60min: "060", 90min: "090", 5h: "300", 10h: "600" }
    write:
      send: ":01SF{value}\r"
  auto_brightness:
    type: boolean
    label: Automatic Brightness
    help: Adjusts the screen brightness to the light in the space.
    state_key: auto_brightness
    default: false
    setup: false
    map: { "true": "002", "false": "001" }
    write:
      send: ":01SR{value}\r"
  ir_remote:
    type: boolean
    label: IR Remote
    help: Lets the display take commands from its infrared remote.
    state_key: ir_remote
    default: true
    setup: false
    map: { "true": "000", "false": "001" }
    write:
      send: ":01SB{value}\r"
  mic_array_alt:
    type: boolean
    label: Microphone Array (code 51)
    help: SMART's code list has two microphone-array codes with the same description. The Microphone Array commands use code 47; this setting writes code 51.
    state_key: mic_array_alt
    default: true
    setup: false
    map: { "true": "001", "false": "000" }
    write:
      send: ":01SQ{value}\r"

quick_actions: [power_on, power_off, mute_on, mute_off]

# Each reply names the code it answers. First match wins.
responses:
  - match: '^:01r0(00[012])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: power, map: { "000": ready, "001": "on", "002": powersave } }
  - match: '^:01r:(001|002|021|103|104|105)$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: input, map: { "001": hdmi1, "002": hdmi2, "021": hdmi3, "103": ops1, "104": usbc1, "105": usbc2 } }
  - match: '^:01r8(\d{3})$'
    set: { volume: "$1" }
  - match: '^:01r9(00[01])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: mute, map: { "001": "true", "000": "false" } }
  - match: '^:01rG(00[01])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: mic_array, map: { "001": "true", "000": "false" } }
  - match: '^:01rQ(00[01])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: mic_array_alt, map: { "001": "true", "000": "false" } }
  # Treble, bass and balance: a sign and two digits; a plain three-digit zero
  # is read too.
  - match: '^:01r1([+-]\d{2}|\d{3})$'
    set: { treble: "$1" }
  - match: '^:01r2([+-]\d{2}|\d{3})$'
    set: { bass: "$1" }
  - match: '^:01r3([+-]\d{2}|\d{3})$'
    set: { balance: "$1" }
  - match: '^:01r4(\d{3})$'
    set: { contrast: "$1" }
  - match: '^:01r\?(\d{3})$'
    set: { backlight: "$1" }
  - match: '^:01r@(00[0-4])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: color_temperature, map: { "000": cool, "001": neutral, "002": warm, "003": very_cool, "004": very_warm } }
  - match: '^:01r<(\d{3})$'
    mappings:
      - group: 1
        state: language
        map:
          "000": english
          "001": french
          "002": spanish
          "004": chinese_simplified
          "006": german
          "007": dutch
          "011": danish
          "013": italian
          "015": norwegian
          "016": finnish
  - match: '^:01rC(00[0-4])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: speaker_output, map: { "000": "off", "001": "on", "002": auto, "003": subwoofer_off, "004": subwoofer_on } }
  - match: '^:01rD(00[01])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: touch, map: { "001": "true", "000": "false" } }
  - match: '^:01rF(000|001|005|010|030|060|090|300|600)$'
    mappings:
      - group: 1
        state: screen_timeout
        map: { "000": never, "001": 1min, "005": 5min, "010": 10min, "030": 30min, "060": 60min, "090": 90min, "300": 5h, "600": 10h }
  - match: '^:01rR(00[12])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: auto_brightness, map: { "002": "true", "001": "false" } }
  # IR is inverted: 000 = enabled.
  - match: '^:01rB(00[01])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: ir_remote, map: { "000": "true", "001": "false" } }
  - match: '^:01rS(00[12])$'
    mappings:
      - { group: 1, state: eco_mode, map: { "002": "true", "001": "false" } }
  # A set's acknowledgement. Its refusal names neither the code nor the value.
  - match: '^401\+$'
    set: {}
  - match: '^401-$'
    set: { last_error: "The display refused a command or a read: a value it does not take, or a setting this model does not have." }

# Every get the code list documents, the controls first. One frame at a time,
# inter_command_delay apart: 19 frames, about 4 s at the default 0.2 s.
polling:
  queries:
    - { send: ":01G0000\r", query_for: power }
    - { send: ":01G:000\r", query_for: input }
    - { send: ":01G8000\r", query_for: volume }
    - { send: ":01G9000\r", query_for: mute }
    - { send: ":01GG000\r", query_for: mic_array }
    - { send: ":01GQ000\r", query_for: mic_array_alt }
    - { send: ":01G1000\r", query_for: treble }
    - { send: ":01G2000\r", query_for: bass }
    - { send: ":01G3000\r", query_for: balance }
    - { send: ":01G4000\r", query_for: contrast }
    - { send: ":01G?000\r", query_for: backlight }
    - { send: ":01G@000\r", query_for: color_temperature }
    - { send: ":01G<000\r", query_for: language }
    - { send: ":01GC000\r", query_for: speaker_output }
    - { send: ":01GD000\r", query_for: touch }
    - { send: ":01GF000\r", query_for: screen_timeout }
    - { send: ":01GR000\r", query_for: auto_brightness }
    - { send: ":01GB000\r", query_for: ir_remote }
    - { send: ":01GS000\r", query_for: eco_mode }

discovery:
  # The documented power get on the protocol's own port: ":01r0" and three
  # digits is this protocol's reply. A display in Power Save answers nothing
  # and is not found.
  tcp_probe:
    port: 4664
    send_ascii: ":01G0000\r"
    expect_regex: '^:01r0[0-9]{3}'

# ── Simulator ──
# Models a T Pro: "401+" for an accepted set, "401-" for a frame out of
# format or a level out of range, an enum code the display does not know
# accepted and ignored (SMART: "Unsupported values may return a Success
# response, but do not result in an actual change in state"), Power On
# answered and ignored unless the display is in Ready, nothing at all answered
# in Power Save, and a change of Eco mode answered and then the network gone
# (the Ethernet toggle in the Simulator UI brings it back). Changes made in
# the Simulator UI are not announced; the driver reads them at its next poll,
# as with the display's own buttons.
simulator:
  # The display answers inside the gap the driver leaves between frames
  # (SMART asks for each reply before the next frame).
  delays:
    command_response: 0.05
  initial_state:
    power: "on"
    input: hdmi1
    volume: 15
    mute: false
    mic_array: true
    mic_array_alt: true
    treble: 0
    bass: 0
    balance: 0
    contrast: 50
    backlight: 80
    color_temperature: neutral
    language: english
    speaker_output: auto
    touch: true
    screen_timeout: never
    auto_brightness: false
    ir_remote: true
    eco_mode: false
    ethernet: true
  controls:
    - type: select
      key: power
      label: Power
      options: ["on", ready, powersave]
    - type: select
      key: input
      label: Input
      options: [hdmi1, hdmi2, hdmi3, ops1, usbc1, usbc2]
    - type: slider
      key: volume
      label: Volume
      min: 0
      max: 30
    - type: toggle
      key: mute
      label: Mute
    - type: toggle
      key: mic_array
      label: Microphone Array
    - type: slider
      key: backlight
      label: Backlight
      min: 0
      max: 100
    - type: toggle
      key: ethernet
      label: Ethernet
  command_handlers:
    # One dispatcher for the whole protocol, because the power state and the
    # Ethernet setting decide whether anything is answered at all.
    - match: ':01[SG].*'
      handler: |
        def _run():
            text = match.group(0).strip()
            if not bool(state["ethernet"]) or str(state["power"]) == "powersave":
                return
            m = re.match(r"^:01([SG])(.)(.{3})$", text)
            if not m:
                respond("401-\r")
                return
            op, code, value = m.group(1), m.group(2), m.group(3)

            LEVELS = {"4": ("contrast", 0, 100), "8": ("volume", 0, 30), "?": ("backlight", 0, 100)}
            SIGNED = {"1": ("treble", 6), "2": ("bass", 6), "3": ("balance", 50)}
            ENUMS = {
                "9": ("mute", {"000": False, "001": True}),
                ":": ("input", {"001": "hdmi1", "002": "hdmi2", "021": "hdmi3",
                                "103": "ops1", "104": "usbc1", "105": "usbc2"}),
                "<": ("language", {"000": "english", "001": "french", "002": "spanish",
                                   "004": "chinese_simplified", "006": "german", "007": "dutch",
                                   "011": "danish", "013": "italian", "015": "norwegian",
                                   "016": "finnish"}),
                "@": ("color_temperature", {"000": "cool", "001": "neutral", "002": "warm",
                                            "003": "very_cool", "004": "very_warm"}),
                "B": ("ir_remote", {"000": True, "001": False}),
                "C": ("speaker_output", {"000": "off", "001": "on", "002": "auto",
                                         "003": "subwoofer_off", "004": "subwoofer_on"}),
                "D": ("touch", {"000": False, "001": True}),
                "F": ("screen_timeout", {"000": "never", "001": "1min", "005": "5min",
                                         "010": "10min", "030": "30min", "060": "60min",
                                         "090": "90min", "300": "5h", "600": "10h"}),
                "G": ("mic_array", {"000": False, "001": True}),
                "Q": ("mic_array_alt", {"000": False, "001": True}),
                "R": ("auto_brightness", {"001": False, "002": True}),
                "S": ("eco_mode", {"001": False, "002": True}),
            }
            POWER = {"000": "ready", "001": "on", "002": "powersave"}

            def wire(table, current):
                for k in table:
                    if table[k] == current:
                        return k
                return "000"

            if op == "G":
                if value != "000":
                    respond("401-\r")
                elif code == "0":
                    respond(":01r0" + wire(POWER, str(state["power"])) + "\r")
                elif code in LEVELS:
                    respond(":01r" + code + format(int(state[LEVELS[code][0]]), "03d") + "\r")
                elif code in SIGNED:
                    respond(":01r" + code + format(int(state[SIGNED[code][0]]), "+03d") + "\r")
                elif code in ENUMS:
                    key, table = ENUMS[code]
                    current = state[key]
                    if isinstance(current, bool):
                        current = bool(current)
                    else:
                        current = str(current)
                    respond(":01r" + code + wire(table, current) + "\r")
                else:
                    respond("401-\r")
                return

            # set
            if code == "0":
                if value not in POWER:
                    respond("401+\r")
                    return
                target = POWER[value]
                # Transition to ON only works while in READY.
                if target != "on" or str(state["power"]) == "ready":
                    state["power"] = target
                respond("401+\r")
            elif code in LEVELS:
                key, low, high = LEVELS[code]
                if not value.isdigit() or int(value) < low or int(value) > high:
                    respond("401-\r")
                    return
                state[key] = int(value)
                respond("401+\r")
            elif code in SIGNED:
                key, limit = SIGNED[code]
                sm = re.match(r"^([+-])(\d\d)$", value)
                if not sm or int(sm.group(2)) > limit:
                    respond("401-\r")
                    return
                level = int(sm.group(2))
                state[key] = -level if sm.group(1) == "-" else level
                respond("401+\r")
            elif code in ENUMS:
                key, table = ENUMS[code]
                if not value.isdigit():
                    respond("401-\r")
                    return
                if value in table:
                    if code == "S" and table[value] != bool(state["eco_mode"]):
                        state["eco_mode"] = table[value]
                        respond("401+\r")
                        # Changing Eco mode turns the display's Ethernet off.
                        state["ethernet"] = False
                        return
                    state[key] = table[value]
                respond("401+\r")
            elif code == "A":
                if not value.isdigit():
                    respond("401-\r")
                    return
                if value == "000":
                    state["volume"] = min(30, int(state["volume"]) + 1)
                elif value == "001":
                    state["volume"] = max(0, int(state["volume"]) - 1)
                elif value == "033":
                    state["mute"] = not bool(state["mute"])
                respond("401+\r")
            elif code == "T":
                respond("401+\r")
            else:
                respond("401-\r")
        _run()
    # Anything outside the frame format.
    - match: '.+'
      handler: |
        if bool(state["ethernet"]) and str(state["power"]) != "powersave":
            respond("401-\r")
